---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Grid Systems — Varve Docs"
  description="Use Varve's document grids, pixel grid, baseline and isometric overlays, and frame-owned layout guides."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Grid systems
      </nav>
      <h1 class="page-hero-title">Grid systems</h1>
      <p class="page-hero-sub">
        Varve keeps document alignment, pixel inspection, typography overlays, and frame layout
        guides separate so each grid says exactly what it does.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Document grid</h2>
        <p>
          Open the Document Grid section in the document inspector. Its visibility, snapping,
          spacing X/Y, subdivisions, offsets, color, opacity, and rotation are independent controls.
          The document grid is saved with the document, so reopening or switching tabs does not
          silently replace its geometry with a local viewport preference.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Show grid</strong> controls the lines.</li>
          <li><strong>Snap to document grid</strong> controls object movement against the lattice.</li>
          <li><strong>Enable snapping globally</strong> is the master switch for all snap sources.</li>
          <li><strong>Grid origin</strong> uses the X/Y offsets; Reset Origin returns both to zero.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Pixel, baseline, and isometric overlays</h2>
        <p>
          Pixel-grid display is a high-zoom view preference and pixel snapping is a separate
          option. Pixel lines use the same camera projection as the document grid, including pan,
          zoom, and view rotation. Baseline and isometric overlays are document-authored settings;
          their configured step, origin, axes, and visibility are respected by the overlay.
        </p>
        <p>
          These overlays are inspection aids. They do not become scene nodes and are excluded from
          exports unless an export workflow explicitly adds grid artwork.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Frame layout guides</h2>
        <p>
          Select a frame and open <strong>Layout guides</strong> below Auto layout. Add one or more
          authored guides, then choose columns, rows, or uniform guides, with count, fixed size,
          gutter, margins, alignment, color, opacity, visibility, and snapping.
        </p>
        <p>
          Layout guides draw boundaries over their owning frame and can provide snap targets for
          its children. They never arrange or resize children. Auto layout's Grid mode, grid
          templates, gaps, and auto-flow remain the separate child-arrangement system.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The document inspector's <strong>Show layout guides</strong> switch is the global view switch.</li>
          <li>Each guide's <strong>Show</strong> switch controls only that authored guide.</li>
          <li>Each guide's <strong>Snap</strong> switch controls only its snap targets.</li>
          <li>Guides outside the active page, canvas, master, or isolation scope are not drawn or offered as snap targets.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>When a guide is not visible</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Confirm the global Show layout guides switch is on.</li>
          <li>Confirm the frame and the individual guide are visible.</li>
          <li>Check that the frame is on the active canvas/page and is not inside a hidden ancestor.</li>
          <li>Check the guide's margins, count, fixed size, and gutter; a geometry that cannot fit its frame is rejected.</li>
          <li>For snapping, enable both global snapping and the relevant guide's Snap switch.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Related guides</h2>
        <p>
          See <a href={sitePath('/features/canvas')}>Canvas & Rendering</a> for the rendering and
          camera model, and <a href={sitePath('/docs/getting-started/interface')}>Interface Overview</a>
          for inspector navigation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
